<DocsLayout title="Analyzers" description="Learn how VulnWeave uses local Gitleaks, Semgrep, OSV-Scanner, and Trivy analyzers.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">REFERENCE</p><h1>Local analyzers</h1><p class="lede">VulnWeave does not replace scanners. It invokes separately installed local tools through small adapters, normalizes their output, and preserves the underlying evidence.</p>
    <h2>Supported tools</h2><div class="command-grid"><code>Gitleaks</code><span>Secret detection. Matched secret values are intentionally excluded from normalized findings.</span><code>Semgrep CE</code><span>Source-pattern analysis using local, reviewed YAML rules.</span><code>OSV-Scanner</code><span>Known dependency vulnerability analysis.</span><code>Trivy</code><span>Filesystem dependency and configuration findings.</span></div>
    <h2>Semgrep rules and packs</h2><p>The starter rules are deliberately small: JavaScript/TypeScript <code>eval</code> plus Python checks for <code>shell=True</code>, redirect-following requests, and disabled TLS verification. Add a reviewed local pack explicitly; packs are additive and each result retains its pack provenance.</p><pre><code>pnpm cli -- . --analyzer semgrep --semgrep-pack typescript-security --format table&#10;pnpm cli -- . --analyzer semgrep --semgrep-pack typescript-quality --format table&#10;pnpm cli -- . --analyzer semgrep --semgrep-pack python-security --format table&#10;pnpm cli -- . --analyzer semgrep --semgrep-pack python-web-security --format table&#10;pnpm cli -- . --analyzer semgrep --semgrep-pack node-web-security --format table</code></pre><p>The TypeScript security pack covers dynamic code and direct child-process checks. The quality pack includes diagnostic <code>console.log</code> checks. The Python pack covers a small set of high-signal source patterns. The web packs add a few focused checks for Python and Node service configuration: debug servers, disabled TLS verification, and unrestricted Express-style CORS. They are not complete framework security audits. Review and tune local YAML before using any pack as a merge gate.</p>
    <h2>Custom Gitleaks policy</h2><pre><code>&#123; "gitleaksConfig": ".gitleaks.toml" &#125;</code></pre><p>Put the setting in the project configuration for portable repository behavior, or use <code>--gitleaks-config .gitleaks.toml</code> for one run.</p>
    <h2>Dependabot-style local coverage</h2><p>VulnWeave checks committed manifests and lockfiles locally, including manifest-only projects such as Python <code>requirements.txt</code>. OSV-Scanner is asked for all known advisories, so the output is designed to cover the same kind of known dependency risks that Dependabot reports—without requiring GitHub dependency alerts to be enabled. A missing or invalid OSV report is shown as failed analyzer health, never as a clean dependency scan. When a scanner reports a fixed version, <code>--format upgrades</code> names the reported manifest and gives a non-mutating, ecosystem-aware next step; it never guesses a version or changes dependencies.</p><h3>Python dependency guidance</h3><p>When OSV-Scanner reports a Python dependency from a supported source path, VulnWeave distinguishes <code>requirements*.txt</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>poetry.lock</code>, <code>Pipfile.lock</code>, and <code>uv.lock</code>. For lockfiles, the recommendation points to the corresponding declaration file and tells you to regenerate the lockfile with Poetry, Pipenv, or uv. VulnWeave does not edit lockfiles and does not claim that every Python package manager or resolver format is covered.</p><h2>Correlation and limitations</h2><p>OSV-Scanner and Trivy can describe the same dependency advisory. VulnWeave correlates matching reports into a canonical issue, retains both pieces of evidence, and uses the highest reported severity. It does not claim a dependency is runtime-safe or unreachable: static import evidence is a review aid only.</p>
  </article>
